    $$python$$,
    $$other$$,
    $$hard$$,
    $$remove_evens$$,
    $$def remove_evens(nums):
    for n in nums:
        if n % 2 == 0:
            nums.remove(n)
    return nums$$,
    $$def remove_evens(nums):
    return [n for n in nums if n % 2 != 0]$$,
    $$remove_evens(nums) should return the list with all even numbers removed.$$,
    $$remove_evens([2, 4, 6, 1]) returns [4, 1] — one of the even numbers survives.$$,
    $$Removing from a list while iterating over it shifts every later element down one position, but the loop's internal index still advances — so the element that slid into the removed slot is skipped entirely. Never mutate a collection you're iterating. Build a new list instead, which a comprehension does cleanly.$$,
    $$[{"input":[[2,4,6,1]],"expected_output":[1]},{"input":[[1,3]],"expected_output":[1,3]},{"input":[[2,2]],"expected_output":[]}]$$::jsonb,
    'published'
  )
  returning id
)
